**Core idea.** Clients depend on an abstraction, never on a concrete source. The concrete source is chosen and injected at runtime.

**Interface (Strategy).**
```
interface DataSource<T> {
  Optional<T> get(String id);
  List<T> query(Criteria c);
  void save(T item);
  void delete(String id);
}
```
Implementations: `DatabaseDataSource` (JDBC or ORM), `ApiDataSource` (HTTP client), `FileDataSource` (JSON/CSV). Each hides its own connection handling, serialisation and error mapping, and throws a common `DataAccessException`.

**Factory.** `DataSourceFactory.create(config)` returns the right implementation from a type key in configuration. Registering creators in a map (`Map<String, Supplier<DataSource>>`) lets you add a new source without modifying the factory (Open/Closed).

**Dependency Injection.** The repository or service takes a `DataSource` through its constructor: `UserRepository(DataSource<User> source)`. A composition root (or DI container) builds the object graph. For *runtime* switching, inject a provider or a `SwitchableDataSource` wrapper (a Proxy/Strategy holder) that delegates to the current strategy held in an `AtomicReference`; `switchTo(newSource)` swaps it atomically. A config watcher or an admin call triggers the switch.

**Layers.** `Service -> Repository (domain-level) -> DataSource (technical)`. Keep the domain objects independent of storage formats; use mappers.

**Concerns.** In-flight requests during a switch (let them finish on the old source), differing capabilities (the file source may not support complex queries, so define the minimum contract or capability checks), transactions and consistency, caching decorator (`CachingDataSource` wraps any source), retries and a fallback source, connection cleanup (`AutoCloseable`).

**Testing.** Use an `InMemoryDataSource` fake and unit-test the service with no I/O.
